1
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:06,398
From Little Britain I went, with my check in my pocket,

2
00:00:06,398 --> 00:00:11,358
to Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant; and Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant,

3
00:00:11,358 --> 00:00:16,078
going straight to Clarriker’s and bringing Clarriker to me, I had the great satisfaction

4
00:00:16,078 --> 00:00:20,917
of concluding that arrangement. It was the only good thing I had done,

5
00:00:20,917 --> 00:00:28,038
and the only completed thing I had done, since I was first apprised of my great expectations.

6
00:00:28,038 --> 00:00:33,957
Clarriker informing me on that occasion that the affairs of the House were steadily progressing,

7
00:00:33,957 --> 00:00:39,000
that he would now be able to establish a small branch-house in the East which was much wanted

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,160
for the extension of the business, and that Herbert in his new partnership capacity would

9
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:50,637
go out and take charge of it, I found that I must have prepared for a separation from my friend,

10
00:00:50,637 --> 00:00:56,480
even though my own affairs had been more settled. And now, indeed, I felt as if

11
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:03,319
my last anchor were loosening its hold, and I should soon be driving with the winds and waves.

12
00:01:03,319 --> 00:01:08,758
But there was recompense in the joy with which Herbert would come home of a night

13
00:01:08,758 --> 00:01:14,319
and tell me of these changes, little imagining that he told me no news,

14
00:01:14,319 --> 00:01:18,879
and would sketch airy pictures of himself conducting Clara Barley to the land of the

15
00:01:18,879 --> 00:01:24,116
Arabian Nights, and of me going out to join them (with a caravan of camels, I believe),

16
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:31,637
and of our all going up the Nile and seeing wonders. Without being sanguine as to my own

17
00:01:31,637 --> 00:01:38,519
part in those bright plans, I felt that Herbert’s way was clearing fast, and that old Bill Barley

18
00:01:38,519 --> 00:01:45,637
had but to stick to his pepper and rum, and his daughter would soon be happily provided for.

19
00:01:45,637 --> 00:01:52,078
We had now got into the month of March. My left arm, though it presented no bad symptoms, took,

20
00:01:52,078 --> 00:01:57,398
in the natural course, so long to heal that I was still unable to get a coat on.

21
00:01:57,398 --> 00:02:02,718
My right arm was tolerably restored; disfigured, but fairly serviceable.

22
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:05,000
On a Monday morning, when Herbert and I were at

23
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,559
breakfast, I received the following letter from Wemmick by the post.

24
00:02:09,559 --> 00:02:15,160
“Walworth. Burn this as soon as read. Early in the week, or say Wednesday,

25
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:21,277
you might do what you know of, if you felt disposed to try it. Now burn.”

26
00:02:21,277 --> 00:02:24,398
When I had shown this to Herbert and had put it in the fire—but

27
00:02:24,398 --> 00:02:29,319
not before we had both got it by heart—we considered what to do. For,

28
00:02:29,319 --> 00:02:33,879
of course my being disabled could now be no longer kept out of view.

29
00:02:33,879 --> 00:02:36,637
“I have thought it over again and again,” said Herbert,

30
00:02:36,637 --> 00:02:42,480
“and I think I know a better course than taking a Thames waterman. Take Startop. A good fellow,

31
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:47,319
a skilled hand, fond of us, and enthusiastic and honourable.”

32
00:02:47,319 --> 00:02:49,839
I had thought of him more than once.

33
00:02:49,839 --> 00:02:52,319
“But how much would you tell him, Herbert?”

34
00:02:52,319 --> 00:02:54,038
“It is necessary to tell him very little.

35
00:02:55,038 --> 00:03:01,519
Let him suppose it a mere freak, but a secret one, until the morning comes: then let him know

36
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:06,957
that there is urgent reason for your getting Provis aboard and away. You go with him?”

37
00:03:06,957 --> 00:03:08,358
“No doubt.”

38
00:03:08,358 --> 00:03:09,917
“Where?”

39
00:03:09,917 --> 00:03:14,277
It had seemed to me, in the many anxious considerations I had given the point,

40
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:23,160
almost indifferent what port we made for,—Hamburg, Rotterdam, Antwerp,—the place signified little,

41
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:27,718
so that he was out of England. Any foreign steamer that fell in our way and would take us

42
00:03:27,718 --> 00:03:33,398
up would do. I had always proposed to myself to get him well down the river in the boat;

43
00:03:33,398 --> 00:03:38,557
certainly well beyond Gravesend, which was a critical place for search or inquiry if

44
00:03:38,559 --> 00:03:45,078
suspicion were afoot. As foreign steamers would leave London at about the time of high-water,

45
00:03:45,078 --> 00:03:50,557
our plan would be to get down the river by a previous ebb-tide, and lie by in some quiet

46
00:03:50,559 --> 00:03:56,480
spot until we could pull off to one. The time when one would be due where we lay,

47
00:03:56,480 --> 00:04:03,917
wherever that might be, could be calculated pretty nearly, if we made inquiries beforehand.

48
00:04:03,917 --> 00:04:07,839
Herbert assented to all this, and we went out immediately after breakfast to pursue

49
00:04:07,839 --> 00:04:14,438
our investigations. We found that a steamer for Hamburg was likely to suit our purpose best,

50
00:04:14,438 --> 00:04:19,438
and we directed our thoughts chiefly to that vessel. But we noted down what other foreign

51
00:04:19,438 --> 00:04:24,480
steamers would leave London with the same tide, and we satisfied ourselves that we knew the build

52
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:31,480
and colour of each. We then separated for a few hours: I, to get at once such passports

53
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:38,277
as were necessary; Herbert, to see Startop at his lodgings. We both did what we had to

54
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:45,519
do without any hindrance, and when we met again at one o’clock reported it done. I, for my part,

55
00:04:45,519 --> 00:04:51,637
was prepared with passports; Herbert had seen Startop, and he was more than ready to join.

56
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:57,038
Those two should pull a pair of oars, we settled, and I would steer; our charge would be sitter, and

57
00:04:57,038 --> 00:05:02,718
keep quiet; as speed was not our object, we should make way enough. We arranged that Herbert should

58
00:05:02,718 --> 00:05:07,078
not come home to dinner before going to Mill Pond Bank that evening; that he should not go there

59
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:12,277
at all to-morrow evening, Tuesday; that he should prepare Provis to come down to some stairs hard by

60
00:05:12,277 --> 00:05:18,199
the house, on Wednesday, when he saw us approach, and not sooner; that all the arrangements with him

61
00:05:18,199 --> 00:05:23,480
should be concluded that Monday night; and that he should be communicated with no more in any way,

62
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:27,358
until we took him on board. These precautions well

63
00:05:27,358 --> 00:05:33,277
understood by both of us, I went home. On opening the outer door of our chambers with

64
00:05:33,277 --> 00:05:40,480
my key, I found a letter in the box, directed to me; a very dirty letter, though not ill-written.

65
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:48,436
It had been delivered by hand (of course, since I left home), and its contents were these:—

66
00:05:48,438 --> 00:05:53,237
“If you are not afraid to come to the old marshes to-night or to-morrow night at nine,

67
00:05:53,237 --> 00:05:58,839
and to come to the little sluice-house by the limekiln, you had better come. If you want

68
00:05:58,839 --> 00:06:03,438
information regarding your uncle Provis, you had much better come and tell no one,

69
00:06:03,438 --> 00:06:09,398
and lose no time. You must come alone. Bring this with you.”

70
00:06:09,398 --> 00:06:16,160
I had had load enough upon my mind before the receipt of this strange letter. What to do now,

71
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:22,680
I could not tell. And the worst was, that I must decide quickly, or I should miss the afternoon

72
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:28,597
coach, which would take me down in time for to-night. To-morrow night I could not think of

73
00:06:28,597 --> 00:06:35,160
going, for it would be too close upon the time of the flight. And again, for anything I knew,

74
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:40,237
the proffered information might have some important bearing on the flight itself.

75
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:46,160
If I had had ample time for consideration, I believe I should still have gone. Having hardly

76
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:51,839
any time for consideration,—my watch showing me that the coach started within half an hour,—I

77
00:06:51,839 --> 00:06:59,917
resolved to go. I should certainly not have gone, but for the reference to my Uncle Provis. That,

78
00:06:59,917 --> 00:07:05,000
coming on Wemmick’s letter and the morning’s busy preparation, turned the scale.

79
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:10,358
It is so difficult to become clearly possessed of the contents of almost any letter, in a violent

80
00:07:10,358 --> 00:07:15,756
hurry, that I had to read this mysterious epistle again twice, before its injunction

81
00:07:15,758 --> 00:07:23,319
to me to be secret got mechanically into my mind. Yielding to it in the same mechanical kind of way,

82
00:07:23,319 --> 00:07:29,160
I left a note in pencil for Herbert, telling him that as I should be so soon going away,

83
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:35,160
I knew not for how long, I had decided to hurry down and back, to ascertain for myself how Miss

84
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:42,160
Havisham was faring. I had then barely time to get my great-coat, lock up the chambers, and make for

85
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:48,237
the coach-office by the short by-ways. If I had taken a hackney-chariot and gone by the streets,

86
00:07:48,237 --> 00:07:53,635
I should have missed my aim; going as I did, I caught the coach just as it came out of the yard.

87
00:07:54,680 --> 00:08:00,597
I was the only inside passenger, jolting away knee-deep in straw, when I came to myself.

88
00:08:00,597 --> 00:08:05,559
For I really had not been myself since the receipt of the letter; it had so bewildered me,

89
00:08:05,559 --> 00:08:11,078
ensuing on the hurry of the morning. The morning hurry and flutter had been great;

90
00:08:11,078 --> 00:08:16,000
for, long and anxiously as I had waited for Wemmick, his hint had come like a surprise

91
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,637
at last. And now I began to wonder at myself for being in the coach,

92
00:08:20,637 --> 00:08:25,000
and to doubt whether I had sufficient reason for being there, and to consider whether I should get

93
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,559
out presently and go back, and to argue against ever heeding an anonymous communication, and,

94
00:08:30,559 --> 00:08:35,559
in short, to pass through all those phases of contradiction and indecision to which I

95
00:08:35,559 --> 00:08:42,917
suppose very few hurried people are strangers. Still, the reference to Provis by name mastered

96
00:08:42,917 --> 00:08:49,319
everything. I reasoned as I had reasoned already without knowing it,—if that be reasoning,—in

97
00:08:49,319 --> 00:08:55,319
case any harm should befall him through my not going, how could I ever forgive myself!

98
00:08:55,319 --> 00:09:00,519
It was dark before we got down, and the journey seemed long and dreary to me,

99
00:09:00,519 --> 00:09:07,237
who could see little of it inside, and who could not go outside in my disabled state.

100
00:09:07,237 --> 00:09:12,519
Avoiding the Blue Boar, I put up at an inn of minor reputation down the town,

101
00:09:12,519 --> 00:09:17,237
and ordered some dinner. While it was preparing, I went to Satis House

102
00:09:17,237 --> 00:09:23,597
and inquired for Miss Havisham; she was still very ill, though considered something better.

103
00:09:23,597 --> 00:09:27,277
My inn had once been a part of an ancient ecclesiastical house,

104
00:09:27,277 --> 00:09:34,199
and I dined in a little octagonal common-room, like a font. As I was not able to cut my dinner,

105
00:09:34,199 --> 00:09:40,398
the old landlord with a shining bald head did it for me. This bringing us into conversation,

106
00:09:40,398 --> 00:09:44,838
he was so good as to entertain me with my own story,—of course with the popular

107
00:09:44,839 --> 00:09:51,116
feature that Pumblechook was my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortunes.

108
00:09:51,116 --> 00:09:53,917
“Do you know the young man?” said I.

109
00:09:53,917 --> 00:09:59,438
“Know him!” repeated the landlord. “Ever since he was—no height at all.”

110
00:09:59,438 --> 00:10:02,038
“Does he ever come back to this neighbourhood?”

111
00:10:02,038 --> 00:10:05,879
“Ay, he comes back,” said the landlord, “to his great friends,

112
00:10:05,879 --> 00:10:10,358
now and again, and gives the cold shoulder to the man that made him.”

113
00:10:10,358 --> 00:10:12,277
“What man is that?”

114
00:10:12,277 --> 00:10:17,319
“Him that I speak of,” said the landlord. “Mr. Pumblechook.”

115
00:10:17,319 --> 00:10:19,758
“Is he ungrateful to no one else?”

116
00:10:19,758 --> 00:10:23,519
“No doubt he would be, if he could,” returned the landlord, “but he can’t.

117
00:10:24,078 --> 00:10:27,796
And why? Because Pumblechook done everything for him.”

118
00:10:27,796 --> 00:10:29,796
“Does Pumblechook say so?”

119
00:10:29,796 --> 00:10:34,597
“Say so!” replied the landlord. “He han’t no call to say so.”

120
00:10:34,597 --> 00:10:36,718
“But does he say so?”

121
00:10:36,718 --> 00:10:42,199
“It would turn a man’s blood to white wine winegar to hear him tell of it, sir,” said the landlord.

122
00:10:42,199 --> 00:10:48,319
I thought, “Yet Joe, dear Joe, you never tell of it. Long-suffering and loving Joe,

123
00:10:48,319 --> 00:10:53,557
you never complain. Nor you, sweet-tempered Biddy!”

124
00:10:53,559 --> 00:10:58,078
“Your appetite’s been touched like by your accident,” said the landlord, glancing at the

125
00:10:58,078 --> 00:11:04,879
bandaged arm under my coat. “Try a tenderer bit.” “No, thank you,” I replied, turning from the

126
00:11:04,879 --> 00:11:11,680
table to brood over the fire. “I can eat no more. Please take it away.”

127
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:16,559
I had never been struck at so keenly, for my thanklessness to Joe, as through the brazen

128
00:11:16,559 --> 00:11:23,758
impostor Pumblechook. The falser he, the truer Joe; the meaner he, the nobler Joe.

129
00:11:23,758 --> 00:11:29,758
My heart was deeply and most deservedly humbled as I mused over the fire for an hour or more. The

130
00:11:29,758 --> 00:11:35,559
striking of the clock aroused me, but not from my dejection or remorse, and I got up and had my

131
00:11:35,559 --> 00:11:42,398
coat fastened round my neck, and went out. I had previously sought in my pockets for the letter,

132
00:11:42,398 --> 00:11:47,718
that I might refer to it again; but I could not find it, and was uneasy to think that it must

133
00:11:47,718 --> 00:11:53,637
have been dropped in the straw of the coach. I knew very well, however, that the appointed place

134
00:11:53,637 --> 00:12:00,480
was the little sluice-house by the limekiln on the marshes, and the hour nine. Towards the marshes I

135
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:04,000
now went straight, having no time to spare. Chapter 53.
